short_text = "Cognitive Load Theory proposes that working memory has limited capacity, and instructional design should avoid overwhelming it. There are three main types of cognitive load: intrinsic load (complexity inherent to the material), extraneous load (caused by poor presentation), and germane load (mental effort devoted to learning). Effective instruction reduces extraneous load by simplifying visuals, segmenting content, and aligning text with diagrams. When learners are overloaded, they struggle to encode information into long-term memory. Well-designed systems support attention, reduce split attention effects, and scaffold complexity over time."
